---
/**
 * The ten research fields, and how much of each one anybody could read.
 *
 * The bar is a field's admitted literature; the blue part is the slice with a
 * readable evaluation section. That split is the chart, not a footnote to it,
 * because it is the single most misleading thing about this dataset if left
 * out: reservoir engineering has by far the most admitted sources and three of
 * them are readable, so a plain count of admitted sources per field would rank
 * the field the review can say least about at the top.
 *
 * Three fields have no readable core source at all. They are called out above
 * the chart rather than left to be noticed as a missing blue tip, and the
 * wording is the report's: those fields are not empty of literature, they are
 * empty of readable evaluation sections.
 */
import type { Field, ReportSection } from '../lib/types';
import ReportPanel from './ReportPanel.astro';

interface Props {
  fields: Field[];
  total: number;
  sections: ReportSection[];
}
const { fields, total, sections } = Astro.props;

const max = Math.max(1, ...fields.map((f) => f.n));
const pretty = (s: string) => s.replace(/_/g, ' ');
const noCore = fields.filter((f) => f.core === 0);
const readable = fields.reduce((n, f) => n + f.core, 0);

// Both panels use one order — admitted count, descending — so their rows line
// up and the chart on the left can be read against the rate on the right.
//
// Sorting the table by rate was tried and is worse: it puts inversion (250%)
// and geothermal (100%) on top, which are taxonomy artifacts the footnote has
// to explain, and buries the actual contrast the section is about — reservoir
// engineering at 92% against seismology at 28%.
const ranked = [...fields].sort((a, b) => b.n - a.n);
const reassigned = fields.reduce((n, f) => n + (f.n - f.from_own_query), 0);
const pct = (v: number | null) => (v === null ? '—' : `${(v * 100).toFixed(0)}%`);
---

<div class="section-head">
  <h2>Research fields</h2>
  <p>
    Every admitted source sits in exactly one of ten fields, assigned by where its
    <em>evaluation</em> is set rather than by what its introduction invokes. The blue
    length is the part of each field that was read in full; the orange length is the part
    known only from an abstract.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="two-up">
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="head">
      <h3>Admitted sources by field</h3>
      <span class="muted">{readable} of {total} readable</span>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <div class="legend">
        <span class="key">core — full text read</span>
        <span class="key context">context — abstract only</span>
      </div>

      <div class="bars">
        {
          ranked.map((f) => (
            <div
              class="bar split"
              title={`${pretty(f.label)}: ${f.n} admitted — ${f.core} core, ${f.context} context`}
            >
              <span class="name">{pretty(f.label)}</span>
              <span class="track" style={`width:${(f.n / max) * 100}%`}>
                {f.core > 0 && (
                  <span class="seg core" style={`width:${(f.core / f.n) * 100}%`} />
                )}
                {f.context > 0 && (
                  <span class="seg context" style={`width:${(f.context / f.n) * 100}%`} />
                )}
              </span>
              <span class="n">{f.n}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="muted" style="margin:.9rem 0 0">
        {total} admitted sources. Bar length is the field's admitted total; the split is
        access, not quality.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="head">
        <h3>What each query family yielded</h3>
        <span class="muted">shortlist → admitted</span>
      </div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 1.25rem">
          Field size is not a function of how much each query returned — it is a function
          of how much of it survived screening. Reservoir engineering does not dominate
          because its query found more; seismology's found more. It dominates because
          almost everything its query found was admitted, most of it as short conference
          papers that are agentic by their own account and unreadable in detail.
        </p>
        <div class="scroll-x">
          <table class="grid">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Query family</th>
                <th class="num">Shortlisted</th>
                <th class="num">Admitted</th>
                <th class="num">Rate</th>
                <th class="num">Core</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                ranked.map((f) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{pretty(f.label)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{f.shortlisted || '—'}</td>
                    <td class="num">{f.n}</td>
                    <td class="num" style="font-weight:600">{pct(f.admitted_share)}</td>
                    <td class="num" style={f.core === 0 ? 'color:var(--faint)' : undefined}>
                      {f.core}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:.9rem 0 0">
          The two counts are different taxonomies and the rate is a ratio between them:
          <em>shortlisted</em> is the query family that <em>found</em> a record, assigned by
          <code>triage.py</code> from title and abstract; <em>admitted</em> is where
          screening put it, by where its evaluation is set. {reassigned} of the {total}
          admitted rows moved between the two, so a rate near or above 100% — inversion,
          geothermal — is that mismatch rather than a perfect yield. Counts come from
          <code>shortlist.md</code>; the per-group figures in <code>RUN.md</code> predate
          the mid-run triage refix and are lower.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panel" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="head">
        <h3>Readable systems, by field</h3>
        <span class="muted">{readable} systems</span>
      </div>
      <div class="body">
        {
          noCore.length > 0 && (
            <p class="claim" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem">
              <span class="tag absent" title="Nothing found, with the queries that looked for it named">
                Absent-searched
              </span>
              {noCore.map((f) => pretty(f.label)).join(', ')}
              {noCore.length === 1 ? ' has' : ' have'} no core-tier source in this run. Those
              fields are not empty of admitted literature — they are empty of readable
              evaluation sections.
            </p>
          )
        }

        <div class="scroll-x">
          <table class="grid">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Field</th>
                <th>Systems read in full</th>
                <th class="num">Core</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                ranked
                  .filter((f) => f.core > 0)
                  .map((f) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{pretty(f.label)}</td>
                      <td>
                        <span class="chips">
                          {f.systems.map((s) => (
                            <button type="button" class="chip" data-system={s}>
                              {s}
                            </button>
                          ))}
                        </span>
                      </td>
                      <td class="num">{f.core}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:.9rem 0 0">
          One row per field with a readable system. Click a system to open its record.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<ReportPanel sections={sections} />
